            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Sin and deep-seated guilt, as well as the typological counterpart of redemption, salvation, and the atoning blood of Christ (often conceptualized as the scarlet thread of redemption).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Isaiah 1:18\",\"Joshua 2:18\",\"Leviticus 14:4-6\",\"Hebrews 9:19\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Earthly wealth, luxury, royalty, and high social status.\",\"basis\":[\"Proverbs 31:21\",\"2 Samuel 1:24\",\"Matthew 27:28\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Spiritual adultery, corruption, persecution of saints, and the violent, anti-God world system.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 17:3-4\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Scarlet is widely recognized in Christian symbolism as having a dual typological nature: representing both the deep, indelible stain of human sin and the means of cleansing through Christ's blood, famously represented by Rahab's scarlet cord. In historical and apocalyptic biblical literature, it additionally symbolizes earthly luxury, wealth, and spiritual harlotry.\"}",
